<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gwandu Emirate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gwandu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gwandu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> was the western half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>, governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullahi-dan-fodio">Abdullahi dan Fodio</a> and his descendants after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> was divided between him and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bello">Muhammad Bello</a>. It oversaw emirates in the western part of the caliphate. It was absorbed into British and French colonial territories during the caliphate's decline at the start of the twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gwandu-emirate.jpg" alt="Gwandu Emirate" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AbdurRahman AbdulMoneim · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sokoto_Sultanate_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the jihadists took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birnin-kebbi">Birnin Kebbi</a>, capital of the Kebbi Kingdom, in 1805, they made the Kebbi town of Gwandu their main base: a small, unfortified settlement founded by the 16th-century Kebbi ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammadu-kanta">Kanta Kotal</a> and used by his successors as a royal farm, set in a valley among defensible ridges. Late in 1805 an alliance led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> and Kebbi defeated the jihadists at Alwassa, but they held Gwandu through five days of fighting and routed their attackers, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bello">Muhammad Bello</a> walled the town.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> ended in 1808 with the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alkalawa">Alkalawa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> left his brother and wazir Abdullahi to carry the fighting westward, and by 1812 he had divided authority in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, giving Abdullahi the western territories. These later reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilorin">Ilorin</a> in the south and Liptako in the west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After Usman's death revolts broke out. Abd al-Salam, who felt cheated in the division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>, was defeated by Caliph Bello in 1818, but his followers held Kalambaina near Gwandu until around 1821. Abdullahi then recognised Bello as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Amir al-Mu'minin</a> and Bello recognised him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of Gwandu, and the two towns became the twin capitals, which has led some scholars to call the caliphate a dual empire. Abdullahi gathered scholars, poets and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a> at Gwandu and left campaigning to his son Muhammad Wani.</p><p>Through the 19th century Gwandu fought Kebbi and its successor state Argungu in the Kebbi Wars. Abuses by Gwandu agents, who seized grain and animals and at times enslaved children, ended a peace that had lasted from 1831 to 1849. A treaty of 1868, the Lafiyar Toga, broke down in the Giru War after 1873, and fighting went on until British troops occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> in 1903, meeting no resistance; much of its former territory fell under French West Africa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Gwandu's statecraft followed Abdullahi's ideal of an Islamic state in which knowledge and piety governed appointments. Succession went by age and seniority, unlike in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a>: the most senior eligible prince was named Dan Galadima, the heir apparent, who also led the army. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> confirmed each emir with a robe, cloak and turban, but since the Dan Galadima succeeded automatically the confirmation was largely ceremonial and kept Sokoto out of Gwandu's affairs. In official correspondence the emir was styled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> al-gharb, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of the West.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gwandu Emirate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A second seat of authority at <b>Gwandu</b>, held by Abdullahi and his descendants, oversaw the western emirates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
